  "live_records": {
    "q-257202": {
      "dom_id": "step-q257202",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+x.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">$[$</td>\n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">\n$2$\n</td>\n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">$]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$</td>\n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" align=\"left\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">\n$-4$\n</td>\n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{H}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-∇f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6xy-4x+1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4y\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy-4x+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-4y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy-4x+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-4y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6y-4 & 6x \\\\ 6x & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}6y-4 & 6x \\\\ 6x & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}6xy-4x+1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}6y-4 & 6x \\\\ 6x & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}6xy-4x+1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6(1)-4 & 6(1) \\\\ 6(1) & -4\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}6(1)(1)-4(1)+1 \\\\ 3(1)^{2}-4(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
