    "q-257219": {
      "dom_id": "step-q257219",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}-8.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-2,\\,-2]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[-1.4,\\,0.4]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">${x}_{2}≈[$</td>\n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">\n$-0.07$\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}-8) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}-8)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4x+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2xy+10y\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x+{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+10y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x+{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+10y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x+10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x+10\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}-4x+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2xy+10y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x+10\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}-4x+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2xy+10y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.4 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2(0.4) \\\\ 2(0.4) & 2(-1.4)+10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-4(-1.4)+(0.4)^{2} \\\\ 2(-1.4)(0.4)+10(0.4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.4 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.8 & 7.2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}5.76 \\\\ 2.88\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.4 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot (7.2)-(0.8)\\cdot (0.8)}[\\begin{aligned}7.2 & -0.8 \\\\ -0.8 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5.76 \\\\ 2.88\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.4 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{29.44}[\\begin{aligned}39.168 \\\\ -16.128\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.07 \\\\ -0.148\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
