    "q-257249": {
      "dom_id": "step-q257249",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-1,2]\\times [-2,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[2,\\,-2]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[1.75,\\,0.125]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">${x}_{2}≈[$</td>\n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">\n$-0.301$\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2xy+6x \\\\ 4xy-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{y}^{2}-2xy+6x) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}-2xy+6x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2y+6 & 4y-2x \\\\ 4y-2x & 4x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}-2y+6 & 4y-2x \\\\ 4y-2x & 4x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2xy+6x \\\\ 4xy-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}-2y+6 & 4y-2x \\\\ 4y-2x & 4x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2xy+6x \\\\ 4xy-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.75 \\\\ 0.125\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-2(0.125)+6 & 4(0.125)-2(1.75) \\\\ 4(0.125)-2(1.75) & 4(1.75)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2(0.125)^{2}-2(1.75)(0.125)+6(1.75) \\\\ 4(1.75)(0.125)-(1.75)^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.75 \\\\ 0.125\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}5.75 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}10.093\\,75 \\\\ -2.187\\,5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.75 \\\\ 0.125\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(5.75)\\cdot 7-(-3)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5.75\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10.093\\,75 \\\\ -2.187\\,5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.75 \\\\ 0.125\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{31.25}[\\begin{aligned}64.093\\,75 \\\\ 17.703\\,125\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.301 \\\\ -0.442\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
